-- THE RECURRENCE GUARD — live as of D194. `nu-ana-build.once` proves a ν can be BUILT;
-- this one proves it can be OBSERVED, which is what makes the ν path testable
-- at all. Until it runs, the only thing standing between the ν codegen and a
-- silent regression is someone deciding to read the proofs.
--
-- It is the exact shape the defect had: build a suspension with `ana`, force
-- one layer with `Out`, and read the payload back out. At `K Int` the layer IS
-- the payload, so forcing `mkNu 42` must yield `42` — and the machine gets
-- there by calling the coalgebra block through the ν's second cell, which is
-- `obs-correct-Out`'s content.
--
--
-- Expected: exit code 42.

import I.Linux.Syscalls as S

mkNu : Int -> Nu (K Int)
mkNu = ana id

-- `Out v : ⟦ K Int ⟧T (Nu (K Int))` = `Int` at this functor.
--
-- APPLIED, deliberately. `t-Out-app-infer` is an INFER rule about
-- `RApp (RResolved (gen "Out")) v`, so the applied form is the one the spec
-- describes. Writing it point-free as `force : Nu (K Int) -> Int; force = Out`
-- instead TYPECHECKS AND EMITS NOTHING — the link then fails on an undefined
-- `once_5force`. See D197: that is a real hole, and this test found it.
main : IO Unit
main = exit@S (Out (mkNu 42))
